Carafe bench: a global memory access through a pointer the shader loaded from memory becomes a storage
buffer bound from that pointer. NAK reads a uniform buffer in a descriptor buffer in two loads: the buffer
address from the set (whose address is in c0[56 + 16 * set]), then the data. Eden tracked the data load to the
set pointer in c0 and read the set at offset "buffer address - set address": zeros, logged only as "Storage
buffer tracked without bias". DXVK keeps D3D9 vertex shader constants in set 2 (c0[88]), so every vertex went
to the origin and the game showed only its clear colour. The hardware LDG reads the computed address.
When the low and high address words are elements k and k+1 of one global load whose own address is a constant
buffer pointer, the buffer is indirect: at bind time the pointer plus offset gives the descriptor {u64
address, u32 size}; a size of 0 uses the mapped size. The shader offset is taken from the loaded address.
NVN-biased and direct accesses are unchanged; global memory fallbacks skip indirect buffers.
GPL-3.0-or-later, as the files it changes.

